 # If they ask "How did you troubleshoot it?"

 Use the **progressive narrowing** approach.

 ### Step 1 — Establish the symptom

 > "The first symptom was slow NPM dependency downloads. The same builds were sometimes taking 50 minutes, sometimes timing out, and sometimes completing normally."

 ### Step 2 — Check Nexus health

 > "We checked Nexus logs and CPU utilization. There were no obvious application errors and CPU wasn't showing a clear bottleneck."

 ### Step 3 — Compare environments/servers

 > "We compared the behavior between the ALM servers and application servers. NPM installation on four ALM servers completed in approximately four minutes, while the application side remained inconsistent."

 This is an **excellent troubleshooting point** to mention because it shows you didn't randomly restart services.

 ### Step 4 — Restart as mitigation

 > "We restarted the Nexus service to eliminate a possible service-level issue, but the problem persisted."

 ### Step 5 — Identify storage problem

 > "Further investigation showed storage pressure on the Nexus server. We removed unnecessary backup files and increased storage capacity by 2 TB."

 ### Step 6 — Validate

 > "After the storage change, NPM builds completed in around two minutes, so we knew storage was contributing to the performance problem."

 ### Step 7 — Investigate why space wasn't released

 This is a very good interview detail:

 > "But we noticed that deleting the files didn't release the expected disk space. We worked with the Unix/storage team and discovered that a snapshot was holding the deleted data. Removing the snapshot released the space."

 ### Step 8 — Investigate the 500 errors separately

 > "The next day, the symptom changed from slowness to HTTP 500 errors. We analyzed the Nexus logs and identified a specific proxy repository within the group repository."

 ### Step 9 — Mitigate

 > "We removed the problematic proxy repository from the group repository, which restored the affected builds."

 ### Step 10 — Permanent fix

 > "Nexus Support confirmed corrupted zero-length metadata in the proxy repository and recommended strict content validation. We recreated the affected repository and enabled strict content validation."

