 ## One thing I would change in how you present this RCA

 Your original RCA says:

 > **"Root cause for Nexus server storage consumption and high volume of data traffic - In Progress."**

 Be careful with this in an interview.

 Don't claim that you definitively identified the **source of high-volume traffic**, because the RCA says the Unix/CDC investigation couldn't identify the actual source behind the proxy.

 Instead say:

 > **"The exact source of the high-volume traffic was not conclusively identified during the incident. However, we identified the storage snapshot as the reason deleted data was not releasing disk space, and we resolved that condition. Separately, we conclusively identified the corrupted proxy repository and disabled strict validation as the cause of the HTTP 500 behavior."**

 That answer is **honest and technically defensible**.

 ## Your 30-second version

 If the interviewer wants a very quick example:

 30-Second Nexus Incident Answer

A major production issue I handled involved Nexus where NPM builds became very slow and later started returning HTTP 500 errors. Initially, we checked Nexus logs and CPU and found no obvious issue. We compared downloads across servers and then identified storage pressure on the Nexus server. After cleanup and a 2 TB storage expansion, build performance improved, but we discovered that an NFS snapshot was retaining the deleted data, so the snapshot had to be removed to release the space.

 The following day, we investigated the HTTP 500 errors and identified a problematic proxy repository in the NPM group repository. We isolated it as an immediate workaround and then worked with Nexus Support. They identified corrupted zero-length metadata and recommended enabling strict content validation. We recreated the affected repository and enabled validation, which provided the permanent fix.

 This incident taught me to separate immediate mitigation from root-cause analysis and to troubleshoot systematically across the application, infrastructure, storage, and repository layers.

 **The strongest keywords to naturally use in your interview are:** _Nexus, NPM, proxy repository, group repository, HTTP 500, storage pressure, NFS snapshot, corrupted metadata, strict content validation, mitigation, permanent fix, vendor RCA, log analysis, cross-team coordination, and post-resolution validation._
